/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1039 "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1045 "The Candidates Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186064994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b309af6c-4786-5404-a3b4-0a5fc0d4a4ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1061 12797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b5f6fdc-d0a8-5c91-a879-4f798a936a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1061 12798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8149edd7-e4fe-579d-804f-4e9bf9911f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1061 12799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5743e9b2-be38-579c-8843-93e55b3e92f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to use the first derivative test to find the local extrema of a continuous function. But how do we find the global extrema of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]?$\n\nWe apply the **candidates test**, which consists of the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1**:  Find all of the critical points. The critical points are our \"candidates.\"\n\n- If the function is defined on a closed interval $[a,b],$ then we must remember to include the endpoints as well.\n\n- If the function is defined on an open interval $(a,b),$ then we do not include the endpoints.\n\n**Step 2**: Determine the value of the function at each of the critical points.\n\n**Step 3**: Compare all the values of $f$ found in step $2.$ The greatest of these values is the global maximum value, and the least is the global minimum value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1065 2667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e714b234-f163-5ae5-a064-6bef2d3b9fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290778 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290778 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290778 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2225758d-8dde-578c-a547-500b65f253b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290887 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290887 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290887 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d7ed184-a058-5189-a723-395001836891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291023 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291023 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a80c147-1f17-5590-baeb-78228c37c97c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291154 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291154 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291154 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7bb02329-a57e-526d-989d-b162a90d0227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291549 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db46b9ef-b0f7-5c48-b877-de4aa36c374a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186290778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186290887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186291154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291869 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "26b8e670-86de-53ef-b5db-4451381e1cb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on the closed interval $4\\le x\\le 9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a126432-a7f1-5215-9a02-985aac25ece8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292804 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x>0.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=4,9$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ f(9) & =\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(9)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8badccb8144447b5b57157308b5614293a54569f8247320a358dd20650d91a1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
